  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nNorthwest flow is in place across our region on the back side \nof the upper low now centered over Hudson Bay. Shortwave ridging\nmoves over the region Thursday followed by southwest flow ahead\nof deepening troughing over the Northern Rockies and Pacific \nNorthwest. After a near seasonable day today, much warmer \ntemperatures return Thursday and Friday over much of the region,\nthough there may be a larger spread in temperatures Friday due \nto the arrival and timing of a strong cold front. This front \nFriday brings the next chance for organized thunderstorms to \nparts of the region (mainly MN). Much more seasonal or even \nbelow average temperatures return this weekend into next week \nbehind that front and lowering heights in response to several \ndeepening upper lows in Canada and the Pacific NW that will \nmigrate over our region. \n\n...Severe Risk Friday Afternoon and Evening...\n\nSoutherly flow ahead of the cold front Friday brings increased \nBL moisture (Tds 62-68F) and a narrow corridor of higher \ninstability (MLCAPE 2000-3500 J/KG) in response to steepening \nmid level lapse rates (7.5-8.5 C/km). Increased mid level flow \nwill bring effective shear values in excess of 40kt to much of \nour region, and ahead of the cold front there is a window where \nhodographs are supportive of supercells and even some supportive\nof dominant right moving supercells and tornadoes. The tornadic\nenvironment may remained more capped before forcing increases \nand shear profiles change in in our area making the threat more \nconditional. Ultimately, the location, coverage, and impacts of \nsevere thunderstorm activity will be determined by the cap \nweakening and the frontal timing during the afternoon/evening. \nEnsemble/deterministic guidance have zeroed in at this range on \na timing favoring mainly our western MN/north central MN \ncounties. This is also reflected in AI based systems which show \nstrong signals for severe impacts in those regions. The ceiling \non impacts for this event includes s significant severe impacts \nfrom hail (2\"+), a conditional risk for tornadoes, and damaging \nwind gusts to 70 mph. though there are enough uncertainties that\nthere are equal or greater chances impacts end up lower due to \nhow narrow the window/corridor for the higher end of parameters \nwill be. This is certainly a set-up to monitor. \n\n...Soaking Rainfall Sunday night into Monday night...\n\nThere is strong consensus that a strong upper low will bring \nwidespread rainfall to our region Sunday night through Monday. \nNBM probabilities are 60-80% for greater than 0.5\" rainfall in \nour CWA during that period, and probabilities for greater than \n1\" of rain range from 40-60% (highest in the southern RRV). \nWhile it is an organized synoptic system, the locations that \nreceive 1\"+ or even 2\"+ rainfall (30% chance) will be determined\nby deformation zone location and embedded thunderstorm activity\nwhich will be less predictable. As the warmer more unstable air\nmass will be cut off to the south of this system there is \ncurrently not a signal for severe thunderstorm risk after \nFriday.",
